    "bio": "a Representative from New York; born in Brooklyn, Kings County, N.Y., November 8, 1879; attended private and public schools; was graduated from Bryant Stratton College, Brooklyn, N.Y., in 1906 and from Pratt Institute, Brooklyn, N.Y., in 1908; engaged in architectural pursuits in 1908; commissioner of highways, Brooklyn, N.Y., 1926-1933; delegate to the State constitutional convention in 1938; elected as a Democrat to the Seventy-seventh and to the five succeeding Congresses (January 3, 1941-January 3, 1953); was not a candidate for renomination in 1952; architect; died on January 27, 1967, in Long Beach, N.J.; interment in Holy Cross Cemetery, Brooklyn, N.Y.",
